---
import Presentation from "../../../../components/Presentation.astro";
import Divider from "../../../../components/ui/Divider.astro";
import Layout from "../../../../layouts/Layout.astro";
import { Image } from "astro:assets";
import TargetImage from "../../../../assets/courses/ap-csp/img/lab-2-4-target.png";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
import { Code as CodeSnippet } from "astro:components";

//#region CodeSnippets
const Figure1 = `ellipse(100, 100, 60, 60);
ellipse(200, 100, 60, 60);
ellipse(300, 100, 60, 60);`;

const Figure2 = `function drawDot(x)
{
  ellipse(x, 100, 60, 60);
}

function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  drawDot(100);
  drawDot(200);
  drawDot(300);
}`;

const Figure3 = `function drawShape(x, y, size)
{
  ellipse(x, y, size, size);
}

function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();
  fill(120, 200, 255);

  drawShape(100, 100, 40);
  drawShape(200, 200, 90);
  drawShape(300, 300, 150);
}`;

const Task1 = `function drawShape(x, y, size)
{
  ellipse(x, y, size, size);
}

function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();
  fill(120, 200, 255);

  drawShape(100, 100, 40);
  drawShape(200, 200, 90);
  drawShape(300, 300, 150);
}
`;

const Task2 = `function drawTree(x, y, size)
{
  fill(90, 60, 40);
  rect(x - size / 10, y, size / 5, size);

  fill(60, 160, 90);
  ellipse(x, y, size, size);
}

function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();

  drawTree(80, 250, 60);
  drawTree(200, 220, 110);
  drawTree(330, 260, 45);
}
`;

const Task3 = `function drawTree(x, y, size)
{
  fill(90, 60, 40);
  rect(x - size / 10, y, size / 5, size);

  fill(60, 160, 90);
  ellipse(x, y, size, size);
}

function setup()
{
  createCanvas(400, 400);
  background(30);
  noStroke();

  drawTree(80, 250, 60);
  drawTree(200, 220, 110);
  drawTree(330, 260, 45);

  // Add a fourth parameter to drawTree for the leaf color
  //   it should take three numbers, or one at a time - your call
  // Draw a row of five trees where each is a different color
  // Make at least one of them a different size too
}
`;
//#endregion
---

<Layout>
  <div class="container my-4">
    <div class="row justify-content-center">
      <div class="col-sm-12 col-md-10">
        <a href="/courses/ap-csp/" class="btn btn-sm btn-outline-light mb-3">
          <i class="si-arrow-left"></i> Back
        </a>

        <h1>Activity 2.4: p5 Lab 7 &mdash; Parameters You Can See</h1>
        <Divider variant="8" classes="mb-5" />


        <!-- What You're Building -->
        <section id="target">
          <h2>What You're Building</h2>

          <div class="text-center my-4">
            <Image src={TargetImage} alt="A dark canvas with three green trees of different sizes, each drawn by the same procedure" width={400} class="img-fluid rounded" />
          </div>

          <p>By the end of <b>Task 2</b> one procedure draws all three trees. The trunk width is worked out from the size, so scaling a tree scales all of it.</p>

          <p class="text-body-secondary">
            <b>Task 3 is not shown.</b> That one is yours to work out.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />
        <!-- Launch / Concept -->
        <section id="launch">
          <Presentation>
            <section>
              <h2>p5 Lab 7</h2>
              <h3 class="text-sn-theme">Parameters You Can See</h3>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Concepts</h2>
              <p>One Procedure, Many Pictures</p>
              <p>Parameters as Dials</p>
              <p>The Create Task's Core Requirement</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Why This Lab Exists</h2>
              <p>This is the most important lab in the course.</p>
              <p class="fragment">The Create Performance Task requires <b>a student-developed procedure with a parameter that affects what it does</b>.</p>
              <p class="fragment">In a terminal you have to take that on trust. Here you can watch it happen.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Circles the Slow Way</h2>
              <Code code={Figure1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Repeated by Hand" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Same shape, one number apart. You have seen this smell twice already.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>With a Parameter</h2>
              <Code code={Figure2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="One Blank" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment"><code>x</code> is a dial. Turn it, the circle moves.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Three Dials</h2>
              <Code code={Figure3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="Position and Size" showLineNumbers={false} />
              <p class="fragment">Three arguments, three completely different circles.</p>
              <p class="fragment"><b>Nothing about the procedure changed between calls.</b> Only what you passed in.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>This Is the Requirement</h2>
              <p>A parameter that <i>affects functionality</i> means changing it changes the result.</p>
              <p class="fragment">A parameter you accept and ignore does not count.</p>
              <p class="fragment">On the canvas, that distinction is impossible to fake.</p>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Today's Objectives</h2>
              <ul>
                <li>Writing a p5 procedure that takes position and size parameters</li>
                <li>Predicting what a call will draw before running it</li>
                <li>Explaining what makes a parameter affect functionality</li>
                <li>Building one procedure that produces visibly different results</li>
              </ul>
            </section>

            <section>
              <h2>Key Terms</h2>
              <div class="text-start">
                <dl>
                  <dt>Parameter</dt>
                  <dd>A named blank in the definition.</dd>
                  <dt>Affects functionality</dt>
                  <dd>Changing the argument changes what the procedure produces.</dd>
                  <dt>Student-developed procedure</dt>
                  <dd>One you wrote. <code>setup()</code> and <code>draw()</code> do not count.</dd>
                </dl>
              </div>
            </section>

          </Presentation>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Build -->
        <section id="build">
          <h2>Build</h2>

          <p>
            Create a new <b>p5.js sketch</b> named{" "}
            <b class="text-sn-theme">2-4-drawshape</b>.
          </p>

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 1: Three Dials</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Create the sketch below and run it.</li>
            <li>Change the third argument of the middle call to <code>200</code>. Run it.</li>
            <li><b>You edited one number and the picture changed.</b> That is the whole idea.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task1} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="p5 Lab 7" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 2: Something Worth Drawing</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Replace it with the tree sketch and run it.</li>
            <li>Notice the trunk width is worked out <i>from</i> <code>size</code>, not typed in.</li>
            <li>Change one tree's size and confirm the trunk scales with it.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task2} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="p5 Lab 7" class="mt-3" />

          <hr class="my-4" />

          <h3>Task 3: Your Own Dial</h3>
          <ul>
            <li>Fill in the comment block. Add a color parameter and draw a row of five.</li>
          </ul>
          <Code code={Task3} frame="code" lang="javascript" title="p5 Lab 7" class="mt-3" />

          <h5 class="mt-4">Challenge (optional)</h5>
          <ul>
            <li>Add a parameter that changes the shape's <i>form</i> rather than its size or place &mdash; a number that decides whether the top is a circle or a triangle. That is a parameter affecting functionality in the strongest sense.</li>
          </ul>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Checkpoint -->
        <section id="checkpoint">
          <h2>Checkpoint</h2>

          <p>
            Your sketch should draw several trees from <b>one</b> procedure, differing
            in position, size, and color.
          </p>

          <p>
            <b>The check is not how it looks.</b> Delete one call and exactly one tree
            should disappear. Change one argument and exactly one thing about one
            tree should change. If that is not true, something is hard-coded.
          </p>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Reflection -->
        <section id="reflection">
          <h2>Reflection</h2>

          <p>Answer these before submitting your work.</p>

          <ol>
            <li>Which of your parameters <b>affects functionality</b>, and how would you prove it to someone in ten seconds?</li>
            <li><code>setup()</code> is a procedure and you did not write it. Why does the Create task not count it as student-developed?</li>
            <li>You will need a procedure like this in Quarter 3. Write one sentence describing a procedure you might want for a project of your own.</li>
          </ol>
        </section>

        <Divider variant="8" classes="my-5" />

        <!-- Submit -->
        <section id="submit">
          <h2>Submit</h2>

          <p>Submit the required files to the appropriate dropbox.</p>

          <h1
            class="text-center mt-5 text-sn-theme border border-primary slow-flash"
          >
            Activity Complete
          </h1>
        </section>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>
